-- The daily salt for ANONYMOUS caller keys (src/guardrails/mcpCallerLimits.js). An anonymous caller is keyed
-- by its client address; an unsalted hash of an address is reversible by enumeration (the IPv4 space is
-- small), so the counter key in gateway_caller_limit_counters and every log tag is an HMAC under the day's
-- random 32-byte salt instead.
--
-- The salt is GENERATED BY THE SERVER inside the INSERT (two random UUIDs, 244 random bits, hex-decoded to
-- 32 bytes), so it is never a bind parameter and cannot reach a statement log (log_min_duration_statement,
-- log_statement, pgaudit print bind parameters). The first instance to need a day's salt inserts one
-- (ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING) and every instance reads back the one that won, so the fleet agrees. The
-- read-back is the only place the salt is copied out. Rows older than two days are deleted by the same code
-- path.
--
-- A REGULAR table, deliberately not UNLOGGED: at most three rows, so its WAL is nothing, and a crash or
-- failover must not silently re-key every anonymous bucket mid-day (which would reset every anonymous
-- budget at once, in the middle of whatever incident caused the failover).
--
-- Its own migration (not folded into 064) so an environment that applied 064 earlier still gets it.
-- Boot-safe: IF NOT EXISTS, a new table only, no rewrite of anything existing.
CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS gateway_caller_limit_salts (
  day        date        PRIMARY KEY,
  salt       bytea       NOT NULL CHECK (octet_length(salt) = 32),
  created_at timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now()
);
